  "source_evidence": [
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26, printed page 4, Section 1.1.1, equation (1.10)",
      "anchor": "d/dt ∫_[x₁,x₂] q(x,t) dx = f(q(x₁,t)) − f(q(x₂,t))",
      "observation": "The integral conservation equation relates the time derivative of the conserved quantity in an interval to its physical endpoint fluxes."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26, printed page 4, paragraph immediately after (1.10)",
      "anchor": "Each component of q measures the density of some conserved quantity",
      "observation": "Each component has a density interpretation; the interval total can change only through endpoint flux. Nonconservation, such as chemical reaction, requires source terms."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF pages 26–27, printed pages 4–5, Section 1.1.2",
      "anchor": "provided that q and f(q) are sufficiently smooth; integral conservation law (1.10) is the more fundamental equation",
      "observation": "Sufficient smoothness permits derivation of the differential equation from the integral equation. At discontinuities the differential equation does not hold classically, while integral conservation continues to hold."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27, printed page 5, Section 1.2, first paragraph",
      "anchor": "cell average of q, which is this integral divided by the volume of the cell",
      "observation": "Finite volume methods approximate cell integrals or normalized cell averages. Cell values are modified at each time step by edge fluxes, using numerical flux functions based on approximate cell averages."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27, printed page 5, Section 1.2.1, equation (1.11)",
      "anchor": "q(x,0) = q_l if x < 0, q_r if x > 0",
      "observation": "A Riemann problem consists of the hyperbolic equation with piecewise constant initial data having a single jump, located at zero in the displayed convention."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27, printed page 5, paragraph following (1.11)",
      "anchor": "q_l = Q_{i−1} and q_r = Q_i; information that can be used to compute a numerical flux and update the cell averages",
      "observation": "Neighboring cell averages provide the left and right Riemann states. Solving that problem supplies information usable for numerical flux computation and a cell-average update over a time step."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF pages 27–28, printed pages 5–6, Section 1.2.1",
      "anchor": "typically a similarity solution; eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the matrix A; approximate Riemann solvers",
      "observation": "The source describes typical similarity and wave structure, linear-system solution through eigenstructure, nonlinear extensions, and the practical use of approximate Riemann solvers when exact nonlinear solutions are expensive."
    }
  ],
  "statement": {
    "binders": [
      "q(x,t) is the state, with each component representing a conserved-quantity density; f is its physical flux function. [Printed p. 4, Section 1.1.1, (1.10) and following paragraph.]",
      "x₁ and x₂ are any two spatial points serving as interval endpoints; t is time. The passage gives no explicit time domain or endpoint-order restriction. [Printed p. 4, sentence introducing (1.10).]",
      "Grid cells carry cell integrals or cell averages; i−1 and i index two neighboring cells with averages Q_{i−1} and Q_i. No global index set is specified. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.2 and paragraph following (1.11).]",
      "q_l and q_r are the constant left and right initial states in a Riemann problem, with its initial discontinuity placed at x = 0. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.2.1, (1.11).]"
    ],
    "hypotheses": [
      "The integral balance concerns a conservation law whose interval total changes only through physical endpoint fluxes; nonconservative effects require additional source terms. [Printed p. 4, (1.10) and its explanation.]",
      "Deriving the differential equation from (1.10) requires sufficient smoothness of q and f(q). This smoothness qualification is not imposed on the passage's discussion of discontinuous solutions satisfying integral conservation. [Printed pp. 4–5, Section 1.1.2.]",
      "For the local finite volume workflow, Q_{i−1} and Q_i are cell averages in neighboring cells and the Riemann data are set to q_l = Q_{i−1} and q_r = Q_i. [Printed p. 5, paragraph following (1.11).]",
      "The Riemann problem includes the hyperbolic equation together with the initial data, rather than initial data alone. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.2.1, opening definition.]"
    ],
    "conclusions": [
      "For the stated conservation law, d/dt ∫_[x₁,x₂] q(x,t) dx = f(q(x₁,t)) − f(q(x₂,t)): the left endpoint flux enters with a plus sign and the right endpoint flux with a minus sign. [Printed p. 4, (1.10).]",
      "At a discontinuity in q the differential equation does not hold in the classical sense, but the integral conservation law continues to hold. Discontinuities can develop even from smooth initial data. [Printed pp. 4–5, Section 1.1.2.]",
      "Finite volume methods use integral conservation, approximate cell integrals or normalized cell averages, and modify these values at each time step through cell-edge fluxes. Their numerical flux functions approximate physical fluxes using the approximate cell averages available. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.2, first paragraph.]",
      "Solving the Riemann problem with the neighboring cell averages as left and right states provides information that can be used to compute a numerical flux and update cell averages over a time step. [Printed p. 5, paragraph following (1.11).]",
      "For hyperbolic problems the Riemann solution is described as typically depending on x/t alone and consisting of a finite set of waves propagating away from the origin with constant wave speeds. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.2.1, paragraph following (1.11).]",
      "For linear hyperbolic systems, the Riemann problem is solved using eigenvalues and eigenvectors of A. The discussion extends the simple structure and construction of exact solutions or arbitrarily good approximations to nonlinear systems such as the Euler equations, and notes that approximate Riemann solvers are used in numerical implementations because exact nonlinear solutions are often expensive. [Printed pp. 5–6, Section 1.2.1.]"
    ],
    "definitions_and_conventions": [
      "A cell average is the integral of q over the cell divided by the cell volume; it is distinguished from both an unnormalized integral and a pointwise grid value. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.2, first paragraph.]",
      "Riemann initial data are q_l on x < 0 and q_r on x > 0 at t = 0. Equation (1.11) leaves the value at x = 0 unspecified. [Printed p. 5, (1.11).]",
      "The choice x = 0 for the initial jump is introduced by 'say', indicating a chosen location for describing the Riemann problem. [Printed p. 5, sentence introducing (1.11).]",
      "The physical flux f in the conservation equation and numerical flux functions in the finite volume method have distinct roles: the latter approximate the former from available approximate cell averages. [Printed p. 4, (1.10); printed p. 5, Section 1.2.]"
    ],
    "implicit_context": [
      "The selected text is an introductory description of conservation laws and a numerical-method workflow, rather than an enclosing numbered theorem giving a quantified numerical guarantee. [Printed pp. 4–5, Sections 1.1.2–1.2.1 and displayed equations (1.10)–(1.11).]",
      "The local workflow is presented with one spatial variable and two neighboring cell states. The subsequent discussion explicitly calls the Riemann problem inherently one-dimensional. [Printed p. 5, (1.11) and following paragraph; printed p. 6, Section 1.3, opening sentence.]"
    ],
    "semantic_facets": [
      {
        "topic": "Physical conservation and normalization",
        "details": [
          "The conserved object is the interval integral of a density, and its change is controlled by physical endpoint flux differences. [Printed p. 4, (1.10) and following explanation.]",
          "Finite volume state values are normalized by cell volume when represented as averages. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.2, definition of cell average.]"
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Information flow at an interface",
        "details": [
          "The source specifies the sequence neighboring averages → left/right Riemann data → solution information → numerical flux → cell-average update. [Printed p. 5, paragraph following (1.11).]",
          "The source does not identify a particular representation of the intermediate solution information or give an explicit update formula in this passage. [Printed p. 5, paragraph following (1.11), qualitative description of information and update.]"
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Strength and scope of the claims",
        "details": [
          "The similarity-solution description is qualified by 'typically'. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.2.1.]",
          "Numerical fluxes are described as approximating correct fluxes 'reasonably well'; no error bound is supplied here. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.2, first paragraph.]",
          "Approximate Riemann solvers are explicitly part of the implementation discussion. [Printed p. 6, Section 1.2.1, final paragraph.]"
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "undebatable_constraints": [
    "Preserve the physical endpoint-flux balance and its sign order from (1.10). [Printed p. 4, equation (1.10).]",
    "Preserve division by cell volume in the definition of a cell average. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.2.]",
    "Preserve the assignments q_l = Q_{i−1} and q_r = Q_i for neighboring cells. [Printed p. 5, paragraph following (1.11).]",
    "Do not replace integral conservation for discontinuous solutions with an assertion of classical differentiability at discontinuities. [Printed pp. 4–5, Section 1.1.2.]",
    "Do not strengthen the qualitative Riemann-information workflow into a specified algorithm, unique numerical flux, convergence theorem, or quantitative accuracy guarantee. None is supplied by this passage. [Printed p. 5, Sections 1.2–1.2.1.]",
    "Do not require exact Riemann solves exclusively or turn the qualified typical wave description into an unrestricted universal claim. [Printed pp. 5–6, Section 1.2.1.]"
  ],
  "ambiguities": [
    {
      "issue": "The precise weak interpretation of integral conservation at discontinuities is not specified.",
      "evidence": "Printed p. 4 displays a time derivative and endpoint evaluations in (1.10); printed p. 5, Section 1.1.2, says integral conservation continues to hold at discontinuities.",
      "impact": "The passage alone does not choose trace conventions, an almost-everywhere time interpretation, or a time-integrated formulation."
    },
    {
      "issue": "No initial value is assigned at the jump itself.",
